Z||000000||RobertVol||12-13-2001||07:43 PM||||I just had my clutch replaced (From Archie,Centerforce clutch)works great!<P>After I got my car back though I found that on hard acceleration I had smoke or mist coming from my top vents and through my AC vents when the AC is on.. The guy at the shop says I might have a bad heater core. Can someone explain the symptoms of a bad heater core?<P>It does not affect my performance I dont think. BUt its a pain....<P>What else could it be? <P>Also ..I have noticed in hard acceleration I hear a ticking sound... I thought Manifolds but I just had them buffed and rust removed and bored out with new gaskets. I thought of removing the CAT and running the Straight pipe..whats the thoughts on this? any good things to this? I have heard it breathes better. Any suggestions on this? I hope removing the CAT will help. There maybe an obstruction in the CAT. Its the stock CAT since 1988 so it probably needs to dropped anyway...Thanks for your opinions.......<P><BR>Thanks................<P>------------------<BR>Robert<BR>88GT 56k T TOP  <BR>North Florida Fiero Club<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Untitled-24.jpg">    <P><A HREF="http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros" TARGET=_blank>http://fullspeed.to/NorthFLFieros</A><P><p>[This message has been edited by RobertVol (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||07:57 PM|||| A bad heater core would let a slimy smoke out on your windshield thru your defroster vents. You will smell anti-freeze as well.<P>No heat either.<P>If it gets worse, it will spill all your coolant out!!<P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over, runs smooth, shifts crisp!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||RobertVol||12-13-2001||08:05 PM||||Well I have smelled the anti freeze smell. but I have not lost my coolent yet. <P>hmmmm.....<P>BUT!!!<BR>The slimy smoke thing was coming through my vents and I have seen the fog come through the vents on the top of the dash... But when I turn my AC on it comes through the AC vents...<P>is that the same?<P>I think so and IF so I need to replace my Heater core it sounds like to me....<P>But thanks||||reg||1
Z||000003||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||08:13 PM||||<BR>Change the heater core or your carpet will be a big mess!!!<P><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Bazooka||12-13-2001||08:20 PM||||The Heater Core is like a radiator without the cap. Heated Engine Coolant passes thru it and heat is radiated from its cores. When leaks originate in the cores from corrosion, you will get leaking antifreeze on the front floormat, usually on the passenger side. If you disregard it the windows will fog up whenever you run the engine. A temporary emergency treatment would be to route the engine outlet hose to the engine inlet hose of the heater, thereby removing the heater core from the circuit, temporarily so that you can at least see out the windows. This corrosion is usually caused by NOT changing the antifreeze at the regular intervals (usually 3 years) which would normally allow the rust inhibitors to do their thing. <BR>************************** <A HREF="http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html" TARGET=_blank>http://hometown.aol.com/m34chev/myhomepage/auto.html</A> <BR>**************************<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Bazooka (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||RobertVol||12-13-2001||08:30 PM||||Well that changes things if so, because this is a 88 gt with only 62K on it and its been babied!<P>ALL fluids and changes have been made at all intervels...<P>plus there is nothing on the floor mats at all....<p>[This message has been edited by RobertVol (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||08:45 PM||||Yeah, but it's still 14 years old!!!!<P>The heater core on my truck went, not because of rust, etc. but because of a blown hose connection on the core.<P>My wifes car went without any trace of fluid. But it had the same symptons.<P>I don't know of any other problem that has the symptons you've described.<P>(The wet floor is usually worst case scenario. I KNOW YOU WOULDN'T WANT THAT.)<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bazooka||12-13-2001||08:58 PM||||Robertvol,   Why dont you disconnect and plug your heater hoses temporarily to see if your smoke disappears from the defrost outlets? Process of elimination may tell for sure. <BR>************************<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||LivoniaFieroGT||12-13-2001||08:58 PM||||Take the CAT out.  Cut with a sawsall and replace with straight pipe.  The exhaust will have a definate rumble and gurgle on deacceleration.  I put 150k on my first GT Fiero and it never created any problems.  Just took a trip from Michigan to Florida in my red GT and for 19 hours each way she ran constant and true.<BR>Don't know about performance gains as I rarely beat mine.||||reg||1
Z||000009||REDHOTT88||12-13-2001||09:00 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LivoniaFieroGT:<BR><B>Take the CAT out.  Cut with a sawsall and replace with straight pipe.  The exhaust will have a definate rumble and gurgle on deacceleration.  I put 150k on my first GT Fiero and it never created any problems.  Just took a trip from Michigan to Florida in my red GT and for 19 hours each way she ran constant and true.<BR>Don't know about performance gains as I rarely beat mine.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Sorry Liv, I was still on that heater core thing..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_drunk.gif"> Forgot he asked "other questions"<p>[This message has been edited by REDHOTT88 (edited 12-13-2001).]||||reg||1
